{# ── 3. WHO I HELP ── #}
<section id="how-i-help" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div data-reveal style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7);">
      <h2>Complicated payslips. A first home. The next move.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Most of my clients work in banking, tech, consulting or data. They understand finance perfectly well, just not the odd, opaque world of mortgage credit policy, where two lenders can look at the same payslip and reach opposite conclusions. That's the gap I fill. Deliberately few clients, done properly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="help-grid--cards">
      <article class="index-card" data-reveal>
        <h3>First-home buyers with complicated payslips</h3>
        <p>Base plus bonus. A payslip that looks fine to you and confusing to a credit policy. Lenders treat the same income very differently: some count 80% of your bonus, some count none of it. I know which is which, and which government schemes you're actually eligible for, in plain English.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="index-card" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 80ms;">
        <h3>The next move</h3>
        <p>You bought the first place years ago; now it's the upgrade, the bridge between selling and buying, or releasing equity without wrecking your borrowing power for the move after this one. Structure today with the next five years in mind.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="index-card" data-reveal>
        <h3>Refinancers</h3>
        <p>If your rate hasn't been reviewed in two years, you're almost certainly paying a loyalty tax. Quick health-check, honest answer on whether it's worth moving, including "no, stay put" when that's the truth.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="index-card" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay: 80ms;">
        <h3>Asset &amp; equipment finance</h3>
        <p>Not everything is a mortgage. Car, caravan, business equipment. Quick approvals, simple paperwork, sorted in days rather than weeks. Handy when life (or the business) can't wait for a settlement cycle.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── 4. WHY WOMBAT ── #}
<section id="story" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="why-wombat" data-reveal>
      <div class="why-wombat__media">
        <figure class="polaroid polaroid--run">
          <img src="/images/tom-community.webp" alt="Tom Carr running in Sydney" width="760" height="950" loading="lazy">
          <figcaption>Sydney local. Runner.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="why-wombat__copy">
        <h2>I spent twenty years inside the banks. Now I work for you.</h2>
        <div class="why-block">
          <h3>From global investment banks in London to retail lending in Sydney.</h3>
          <p>Twenty-plus years across analytics, strategy, digital banking, and product leadership at some of the world's largest investment banks, and later Commonwealth Bank in Sydney. I've sat in the rooms where credit policy gets written, and seen exactly how banks decide who's a good risk and who isn't.</p>
          <p>That insider view is the real value. Most of my clients are people like my former colleagues. Sharp, financially literate, and still baffled by why a lender said no. Because most rejection letters aren't "you can't afford this". They're "you applied to the wrong lender for your situation." Knowing the difference is most of the job.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-block">
          <h3>I live where you live. Sydney local. Runner. School volunteer.</h3>
          <p>People I'd want to bump into at Saturday parkrun deserve advice that holds up in daylight. I served as a volunteer non-executive director at the local primary school. None of which is the reason to choose a broker. But it's the reason I built this business the way I did.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-block">
          <h3>I don't disappear at settlement.</h3>
          <p>Every year I check your loan against the market, run the numbers, and tell you whether it's still the right deal. Most years it is. Sometimes it isn't. Either way you'll know.</p>
          <p>And it costs you nothing. Lenders pay brokers for introducing the loan; the borrower never sees a bill. Australia's Best Interests Duty makes that more than a vibe. It's a legal requirement that I act in <em>your</em> interests, not the bank's.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="why-block why-block--origin">
          <h3>“Wombat” comes from the Beecroft Wombats.</h3>
          <p>Our kids soccer team I used to coach. I'm originally from the UK, but Sydney's been home for around fifteen years, and Beecroft for most of those.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── 5. HOW IT WORKS ── #}
<section id="process" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div data-reveal style="margin-bottom: var(--space-7); max-width: 700px;">
      <h2>Four conversations between you and your keys.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Most of the work is mine. Yours is mostly answering questions and signing things.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="notebook" data-reveal>
      <article class="notebook__step">
        <h3>1. The strategy session</h3>
        <p>Thirty minutes on Google Meet. You tell me the situation; I tell you whether it's actually achievable, and roughly with whom. No paperwork yet. Just a strategy.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="notebook__step">
        <h3>2. The deep dive</h3>
        <p>We collect documents, build the file properly, and pre-position the application with two or three lenders that fit. Most brokers send you to one. I want options.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="notebook__step">
        <h3>3. Application &amp; approval</h3>
        <p>I lodge, follow up daily, and translate banker-speak back to you in plain English. You don't need to chase anyone. That's literally my job.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="notebook__step">
        <h3>4. Settlement &amp; beyond</h3>
        <p>Settlement happens. You get the keys. Then I check in every twelve months to see whether your loan is still the right one. For as long as you have it.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

